APX.pt = {"country":{"name":"Portugal","tagline":"A narrow Atlantic country with some 250 native grapes, two of the world's great fortified wines and a table-wine revolution still gathering speed.","classification":"Portugal uses the EU two-tier system with its own labels. The top tier is the DOP (Denominação de Origem Protegida), still almost universally written DOC (Denominação de Origem Controlada) on labels and by wine people; there are 30 wine DOPs registered with the EU (31 if you count Porto and Douro, which share one territory, as two, which the register does). The second tier is the IGP (Indicação Geográfica Protegida), labelled Vinho Regional: Minho, Transmontano, Duriense, Terras de Cister, Terras do Dão, Terras da Beira, Beira Atlântico, Lisboa, Tejo, Península de Setúbal, Alentejano, Algarve, Açores and Terras Madeirenses. Many of Portugal's most ambitious wines are Vinho Regional because the producer wanted to use grapes or blends outside the DOC rules. Below that is plain Vinho (wine without geographical indication).\n\nEach region is policed by a Comissão Vitivinícola Regional (CVR), except the Douro (IVDP) and Madeira (IVBAM), where state institutes control both production and the fortified-wine trade. National policy is set by the Instituto da Vinha e do Vinho (IVV).\n\nLabel terms that matter: Reserva (higher quality, at least 0.5% above the minimum alcohol for the DOC); Grande Reserva; Garrafeira (for red, at least 30 months ageing including 12 in bottle; for white 12 months including 6 in bottle); Colheita (vintage); Quinta (single estate); Herdade (estate, used in the south); Escolha (selection). Port and Madeira have their own category systems, explained in their entries. Some DOCs contain subregions that may be named on the label (Vinho Verde has nine, Alentejo eight, Dão seven, Beira Interior three, Trás-os-Montes three).\n\nTwo quirks: the territory of the Douro carries two DOPs on one map, Porto for fortified wine and Douro for unfortified wines, and growers there need a quota (beneficio) to turn their grapes into Port; and on Madeira the DOP Madeira covers the fortified wine while Madeirense covers the island's young dry table wines."},"regions":[{"name":"Douro Valley","slug":"douro-valley","tagline":"A river carved through schist, terraced by hand over three centuries: the home of Port and of Portugal's most powerful red wines.","ids":["pt-baixo-corgo","pt-cima-corgo","pt-douro","pt-douro-superior","pt-duriense","pt-moscatel-do-douro","pt-porto"]},{"name":"Madeira","slug":"madeira","tagline":"A volcanic island 1,000 km out in the Atlantic whose wine is deliberately heated and oxidised, and is all but indestructible.","ids":["pt-madeira","pt-madeirense","pt-terras-madeirenses"]},{"name":"Vinho Verde","slug":"vinho-verde","tagline":"Portugal's green, rain-soaked north-west, where granite, Atlantic air and high-trained vines make some of Europe's freshest whites.","ids":["pt-amarante","pt-ave","pt-baiao","pt-basto","pt-cavado","pt-lima","pt-minho","pt-moncao-e-melgaco","pt-paiva","pt-sousa","pt-vinho-verde"]},{"name":"Trás-os-Montes","slug":"tr-s-os-montes","tagline":"'Behind the mountains': a remote, high, continental corner of north-east Portugal with old field blends and sharp, mountain-fresh wines.","ids":["pt-chaves","pt-planalto-mirandes","pt-transmontano","pt-tras-os-montes","pt-valpacos"]},{"name":"Távora-Varosa","slug":"t-vora-varosa","tagline":"Portugal's first region demarcated for sparkling wine, on high Cistercian land south of the Douro.","ids":["pt-terras-de-cister","pt-tavora-varosa"]},{"name":"Bairrada","slug":"bairrada","tagline":"Clay, Atlantic air and the fearsome Baga grape: Portugal's Nebbiolo country, and its capital of sparkling wine and roast suckling pig.","ids":["pt-bairrada","pt-beira-atlantico"]},{"name":"Dão","slug":"d-o","tagline":"A granite bowl ringed by mountains, the homeland of Touriga Nacional and of Portugal's most Burgundian reds and whites.","ids":["pt-alva","pt-besteiros","pt-castendo","pt-dao","pt-lafoes","pt-serra-da-estrela","pt-silgueiros","pt-terras-de-azurara","pt-terras-de-senhorim","pt-terras-do-dao"]},{"name":"Beira Interior","slug":"beira-interior","tagline":"Portugal's highest wine region, on the plateaux along the Spanish border, where old vines and altitude make taut, mineral wines.","ids":["pt-beira-interior","pt-castelo-rodrigo","pt-cova-da-beira","pt-pinhel","pt-terras-da-beira"]},{"name":"Lisboa","slug":"lisboa","tagline":"Windswept Atlantic hills north of the capital: once Portugal's bulk-wine engine, now a home of fresh whites, value reds and three historic oddities.","ids":["pt-alenquer","pt-arruda","pt-bucelas","pt-carcavelos","pt-colares","pt-encostas-d-aire","pt-lisboa","pt-lourinha","pt-torres-vedras","pt-obidos"]},{"name":"Tejo","slug":"tejo","tagline":"Portugal's breadbasket on the banks of the Tagus, where fertile floodplains meet sandy heaths and limestone hills.","ids":["pt-dotejo","pt-tejo"]},{"name":"Península de Setúbal","slug":"pen-nsula-de-set-bal","tagline":"Muscat country south of Lisbon: amber Moscatel de Setúbal from the Arrábida hills and supple Castelão from the sandy plain.","ids":["pt-palmela","pt-peninsula-de-setubal","pt-setubal"]},{"name":"Alentejo","slug":"alentejo","tagline":"Big skies, cork oaks and marble towns: Portugal's favourite wine region, and the last place in Europe where wine is still routinely made in Roman-style clay jars.","ids":["pt-alentejano","pt-alentejo","pt-borba","pt-granja-amareleja","pt-moura","pt-portalegre","pt-redondo","pt-reguengos","pt-vidigueira","pt-evora"]},{"name":"Algarve","slug":"algarve","tagline":"Portugal's sunny southern coast, where a small wine revival is growing between the golf courses.","ids":["pt-algarve","pt-lagoa","pt-lagos","pt-portimao","pt-tavira"]},{"name":"Azores","slug":"azores","tagline":"Mid-Atlantic volcanic islands where vines grow in black-stone pens beside the surf: Pico's UNESCO landscape and some of Europe's most saline whites.","ids":["pt-acores","pt-biscoitos","pt-graciosa","pt-pico"]}],"items":{"pt-acores":{"name":"Açores","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Azores","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Azores archipelago, used for most of the islands' modern dry whites, reds and rosés.","rslug":"azores"},"pt-alenquer":{"name":"Alenquer","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"DOP registered 1991 (former IPR)","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz, Castelão, Syrah, Alicante Bouschet; Arinto, Fernão Pires, Vital","summary":"The most prestigious of the Lisboa table-wine DOCs, in a valley sheltered from the ocean by the Serra de Montejunto, which makes it warmer and drier than the coast. Quinta do Monte d'Oiro (whose Syrah, from Hermitage cuttings, put the region on the map in the 1990s) and Quinta de Chocapalha lead a group of estates making supple, well-built reds and fresh whites.","terroir":"Clay-limestone hills behind the Montejunto range, rain shadow, warm days.","producers":["Quinta do Monte d'Oiro","Quinta de Chocapalha","Quinta da Boavista (Casa Santos Lima)","Quinta de Pancas"],"nerd_note":"Quinta do Monte d'Oiro's owner José Bento dos Santos planted Syrah cuttings sourced from Hermitage, and its Reserva is often cited as Portugal's best Syrah.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-alentejano":{"name":"Alentejano","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Alentejo","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation covering the whole Alentejo, used for most of its wine, including many top bottlings that use varieties or blends outside DOC rules.","rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-alentejo":{"name":"Alentejo","cat":"DOC","region":"Alentejo","created":"DOC unifying eight subregions; EU registration 1996","area_ha":26000,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling","orange"],"grapes":"Aragonez, Trincadeira, Alicante Bouschet, Castelão, Touriga Nacional, Syrah; Antão Vaz, Roupeiro, Arinto, Fernão Pires","summary":"The DOC covers eight scattered subregions rather than the whole Alentejo. The classic Alentejo red is a blend of Aragonez, Trincadeira and Alicante Bouschet: dark, plush, warm and spicy, with soft tannins, the style that made the region Portugal's bestseller in supermarkets and restaurants. At the top end, estates such as Mouchão (with Alicante Bouschet since the early 20th century), Esporão, Cartuxa (Pêra-Manca) and the Portalegre growers make wines of structure and longevity. Whites from Antão Vaz (tropical and broad) and Arinto (fresh) have improved enormously.\n\nVinho de Talha is the Alentejo's living Roman heritage: grapes are crushed into large clay jars (talhas), sometimes 1,000 to 2,000 litres, lined with a resin called pez, and fermented with the skins, which sink as the wine clears. By tradition the talhas are opened on St Martin's Day, 11 November. The style was formally recognised in 2010; in 2021, 23 producers made about 180,000 litres, a tiny but fast-growing niche.","terroir":"Hot, dry plains and low hills on schist, granite, marble and clay; the Serra de São Mamede is the cool exception.","producers":["Herdade do Mouchão","Herdade do Esporão","Fundação Eugénio de Almeida (Cartuxa, Pêra-Manca)","Quinta do Mouro","Tapada de Coelheiros","Herdade do Rocim","Herdade dos Grous","Herdade do Peso (Sogrape)","Adega Mayor","Quinta da Fonte Souto (Symington)","Susana Esteban","José de Sousa (José Maria da Fonseca)","Adega de Borba","CARMIM (Reguengos)"],"nerd_note":"Pêra-Manca, Cartuxa's flagship red, made only in exceptional years, takes its name from a 'wobbly stone' (pedra manca) and is said to have been among the wines Pedro Álvares Cabral took to Brazil in 1500.","contains":["Portalegre","Borba","Redondo","Reguengos","Vidigueira","Évora","Granja-Amareleja","Moura"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-algarve":{"name":"Algarve","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Algarve","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Algarve, under which most of its quality estates bottle their wines.","rslug":"algarve"},"pt-alva":{"name":"Alva","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Alfrocheiro, Tinta Roriz; Encruzado","summary":"The southern subregion along the Alva river around Oliveira do Hospital, sheltered by the Estrela and Açor ranges.","terroir":"Granite and schist, river valleys.","rslug":"d-o"},"pt-amarante":{"name":"Amarante","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Avesso, Azal, Arinto; Amaral, Vinhão, Espadeiro","summary":"Around Amarante on the Tâmega, warmer and drier than the coast: Azal and Avesso whites and some of the region's sturdiest reds.","terroir":"Granite, sheltered by the Marão.","rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-arruda":{"name":"Arruda","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"DOP registered 1991 (former IPR)","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Castelão, Tinta Roriz, Touriga Nacional; Arinto, Fernão Pires, Vital","summary":"Around Arruda dos Vinhos ('Arruda of the wines'), just north of Lisbon. A historic source of rustic red for the capital, dominated by its cooperative; easy, fruity reds and whites. The lines of Torres Vedras, Wellington's defensive forts of 1810, cross the area.","terroir":"Clay-limestone hills just inland from the Atlantic.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Arruda dos Vinhos"],"rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-ave":{"name":"Ave","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Loureiro, Arinto, Trajadura, Alvarinho; Vinhão","summary":"The Ave valley around Guimarães, Famalicão and Vila Verde: classic blended whites of Loureiro, Arinto and Trajadura and some of the region's best sparkling bases.","terroir":"Granite; Atlantic influence moderated by distance from the sea.","producers":["Quinta de Covela (nearby)"],"rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-baiao":{"name":"Baião","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Avesso, Arinto, Azal; Amaral, Vinhão","summary":"On the north bank of the Douro next to the Port region's western edge, Baião is the home of Avesso, a full, textured white that ripens well in the warmer, sunnier slopes. Quinta de Covela, revived since 2011, shows how good it can be.","terroir":"Granite slopes facing the Douro; the warmest, driest Vinho Verde subregion, a transition to the Douro climate.","producers":["Quinta de Covela","Quinta de Santa Cristina"],"rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-bairrada":{"name":"Bairrada","cat":"DOC","region":"Bairrada","created":"1979","area_ha":9300,"styles":["red","white","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"Baga, Touriga Nacional, Alfrocheiro, Castelão, Tinta Roriz; Maria Gomes (Fernão Pires), Bical, Cercial, Arinto","summary":"Bairrada makes two very different things brilliantly. Its reds, from Baga on clay-limestone, are among Europe's most structured: high acidity and dense, grippy tannins that need ten or twenty years to unfold into notes of sour cherry, tobacco, tar and forest floor. Traditional producers fermented with stems and aged in large old casks; old Garrafeiras from the 1950s to 1990s are some of Portugal's great bargains. Its sparkling wines, from Maria Gomes, Bical, Arinto and Baga, are crisp and toasty, sold in every price band, and have been made here since the 1890s.\n\nBecause ripening Baga is risky in a wet Atlantic autumn, many producers blended it with Touriga Nacional or international grapes in the 1990s, while purists, led by Luís Pato, Filipa Pato and the Baga Friends, insisted on Baga alone. The Bairrada Clássico category rewards the traditional path.","terroir":"Clay-limestone hills (Jurassic marls) for the great reds; sands for lighter wines and whites; maritime, rainy and cool.","producers":["Luís Pato","Filipa Pato & William Wouters","Quinta das Bágeiras","Caves São João","Sidónio de Sousa","Buçaco (Alexandre Almeida)","Quinta de Baixo (Niepoort)","Kompassus","Aliança","Messias","Campolargo"],"nerd_note":"Luís Pato's Vinha Pan, Vinha Barrosa and Quinta do Ribeirinho Pé Franco (ungrafted Baga on sand) are the closest Portugal has to Barolo crus; he left the DOC for years in protest at its rules and labels most wines Beira Atlântico.","rslug":"bairrada"},"pt-baixo-corgo":{"name":"Baixo Corgo","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Douro Valley","created":"1756 (part of the original demarcation)","area_ha":14000,"styles":["fortified","red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Touriga Nacional; whites Malvasia Fina, Gouveio","summary":"The westernmost and smallest of the three subregions, from Barqueiros to the Corgo river at Peso da Régua and Lamego on the south bank. It is the wettest (around 900 mm of rain at Régua), greenest and most densely planted part of the Douro, about 14,000 ha of vines, with deeper soils and higher yields. Historically it supplied the bulk of young ruby and tawny Port; today it is also home to ambitious table-wine estates such as Quinta do Vallado, Quinta da Gaivosa and Quinta do Côtto, whose wines tend to be fresher and more elegant than those upstream.","terroir":"Schist with granite outcrops; Atlantic influence through the Marão gap; many patamares and vinha ao alto plantings on gentler slopes.","producers":["Quinta do Vallado","Quinta da Gaivosa (Alves de Sousa)","Quinta do Côtto","Quinta da Pacheca","Casa de Mateus (Sogrape, IGP and DOC)"],"nerd_note":"Peso da Régua was the river port where the Companhia's officials weighed and taxed the wine (peso means weight), and it is still the administrative capital of the region.","rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-basto":{"name":"Basto","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Azal, Arinto, Trajadura; Rabo de Anho, Espadeiro, Vinhão","summary":"The inland Tâmega country around Cabeceiras, Celorico and Mondim de Basto, behind the Marão foothills. Hotter summers and colder winters give fuller reds and the zesty, apple-scented Azal whites.","terroir":"Granite and some schist, more continental climate.","rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-beira-atlantico":{"name":"Beira Atlântico","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Bairrada","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation covering Bairrada and the coastal Beiras, used by many top producers (including Luís Pato) for wines outside DOC rules. Formerly part of the Beiras IGP.","rslug":"bairrada"},"pt-beira-interior":{"name":"Beira Interior","cat":"DOC","region":"Beira Interior","created":"EU registration 2004 (merger of three IPRs)","styles":["white","red","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"Síria, Fonte Cal, Arinto, Fernão Pires; Rufete, Tinta Roriz, Touriga Nacional, Marufo, Jaen","summary":"A big, high, sparsely planted DOC along the Spanish frontier. Síria, the local name for Roupeiro, gives citrusy, floral whites with real cut at altitude; Fonte Cal is a local white rarity. Reds from Rufete, Touriga Nacional and Tinta Roriz are lighter and more perfumed than in the Douro next door. Pinhel has a long sparkling-wine tradition; Cova da Beira, between the Estrela and Gardunha ranges around Fundão and Covilhã, has the most dynamic estates.","terroir":"Granite and schist, 300 to 750 m, continental climate.","producers":["Quinta dos Termos","Quinta do Cardo","Rui Roboredo Madeira (Beyra)","Adega Cooperativa de Pinhel"],"nerd_note":"Síria is the same grape as Roupeiro in the Alentejo and Códega in the Douro: one variety, three regional names.","contains":["Castelo Rodrigo","Pinhel","Cova da Beira"],"rslug":"beira-interior"},"pt-besteiros":{"name":"Besteiros","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Baga, Jaen; Encruzado, Bical","summary":"Around Tondela on the western side, toward the Caramulo mountains: wetter and more Atlantic, with lighter, fresher wines. Casa de Mouraz farms organically here.","terroir":"Granite with some schist, more rainfall.","producers":["Casa de Mouraz"],"rslug":"d-o"},"pt-biscoitos":{"name":"Biscoitos","cat":"DOC","region":"Azores","created":"1994; DOP registered 1996","styles":["fortified","white"],"grapes":"Verdelho, Arinto, Terrantez, Boal","summary":"A tiny DOC on the north coast of Terceira island, named after the 'biscuit'-like jagged lava rock on which the vines grow in walled curraletas. It is known for fortified Verdelho, traditionally made by the Brum family's Casa Agrícola, whose museum documents the island's wine history; dry whites are increasing.","terroir":"Rough, porous basalt lava fields beside the sea, sheltered by stone walls.","producers":["Casa Agrícola Brum (Museu do Vinho dos Biscoitos)","Adega Cooperativa dos Biscoitos"],"rslug":"azores"},"pt-borba":{"name":"Borba","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Aragonez, Trincadeira, Alicante Bouschet, Castelão; Roupeiro, Arinto","summary":"Between Estremoz and Vila Viçosa in the 'marble triangle', where the soils are pale limestone and schist over marble. The Adega de Borba cooperative (1955) was one of the first to bottle quality Alentejo wine; Quinta do Mouro near Estremoz is the subregion's star estate.","terroir":"Marble-derived limestone and schist; slightly cooler and higher than the southern plains.","producers":["Adega de Borba","Quinta do Mouro","Quinta do Carmo"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-bucelas":{"name":"Bucelas","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"1907 to 1911 (one of the first demarcations)","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Arinto (dominant), Sercial (Esgana Cão), Rabo de Ovelha","summary":"A small valley 25 km north of Lisbon whose DOC is for white wine only, built on Arinto: a grape with lemon, green apple and lime-blossom aromas and a ramrod of acidity that lets the wine age for a decade. The Duke of Wellington took a liking to it during the Peninsular War, and in Regency London it was sold as 'Portuguese Hock' or 'Lisbon Hock', compared to the Rhine Rieslings of the day. By the 1980s almost all vineyards belonged to a single estate and the name had nearly vanished; Quinta da Romeira, Quinta da Murta and newcomers revived it from the 1990s. There is also a little traditional-method sparkling Bucelas.","terroir":"Limestone and clay marls in the sheltered valley of the Trancão river, with Atlantic winds funnelling through.","producers":["Quinta da Romeira","Quinta da Murta","Caves Velhas","Quinta do Boição"],"nerd_note":"Bucelas is a white-only DOC built on one grape: Arinto must dominate every blend, with Sercial (Esgana Cão) and Rabo de Ovelha in support.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-carcavelos":{"name":"Carcavelos","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"1908","area_ha":32,"styles":["fortified","sweet","white"],"grapes":"Galego Dourado, Ratinho, Arinto, Boal, Seara Nova; Castelão, Preto Martinho, Negra Mole","summary":"Carcavelos is a fortified wine from the coast between Lisbon and Cascais, made by stopping fermentation with grape spirit and then ageing the wine for years in wood: amber, off-dry to medium-sweet, with notes of dried apricot, almond and orange peel and a salty finish from the Atlantic. In the 18th century it was a celebrated export; the Marquis of Pombal owned a vineyard estate in Oeiras and pushed Carcavelos into the English market, and shipments are recorded as far as the courts of Europe.\n\nThen the suburbs came. Phylloxera, war, economic crisis and above all urban sprawl along the Lisbon to Cascais railway reduced the vineyards to almost nothing by the late 20th century. Revival began in 1983, when the Oeiras municipality and the Ministry of Agriculture replanted 5 ha at the Marquis's former estate under the Villa Oeiras project; by October 2024 about 32 ha were growing Carcavelos, with more planned. It remains one of the smallest DOCs in Europe.","terroir":"Limestone and clay, sea-facing slopes beside the Tejo estuary, warm and windy with salt air.","producers":["Villa Oeiras (Municipality of Oeiras)","Casal da Manteiga"],"nerd_note":"Villa Oeiras is a rare case of a city council running a winery: its cellar under the Marquis of Pombal's estate ages the wine, and the project's stated goal is eventually a 100-year-old Carcavelos.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-castelo-rodrigo":{"name":"Castelo Rodrigo","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Beira Interior","subregion":"Beira Interior","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Rufete, Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz; Síria","summary":"The northern subregion around Figueira de Castelo Rodrigo, bordering the Douro Superior and Spain; warm, schist-and-granite hills with a hill-top medieval village. Quinta do Cardo is the best-known estate.","terroir":"Schist and granite, the warmest part of the Beira Interior.","producers":["Quinta do Cardo"],"rslug":"beira-interior"},"pt-castendo":{"name":"Castendo","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz, Jaen; Malvasia Fina","summary":"The north-east of the Dão around Penalva do Castelo (formerly Castendo), on the plateau toward the Beira Alta.","terroir":"Granite, higher and more continental.","rslug":"d-o"},"pt-cavado":{"name":"Cávado","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Loureiro, Arinto, Trajadura; Vinhão, Borraçal","summary":"The Cávado valley around Barcelos and Braga, close to the coast: light, aromatic Loureiro- and Arinto-based whites and fresh reds.","terroir":"Granite, maritime climate, high rainfall.","rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-chaves":{"name":"Chaves","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Trás-os-Montes","subregion":"Trás-os-Montes","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Bastardo, Tinta Roriz, Trincadeira; Côdega do Larinho, Fernão Pires","summary":"The valley of the Tâmega around the spa town of Chaves near the Spanish border: light, fresh reds and palhetes from granite soils, historically an IPR in its own right.","terroir":"Granite valley at 350 to 500 m, colder and wetter than the rest of the DOC.","rslug":"tr-s-os-montes"},"pt-cima-corgo":{"name":"Cima Corgo","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Douro Valley","created":"1756 (core extended in 1761)","area_ha":19000,"styles":["fortified","red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Franca, Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz, Tinto Cão, Tinta Barroca, Sousão, field blends","summary":"The heart of Port: from the Corgo river east to the Cachão da Valeira gorge, centred on the village of Pinhão and the side valleys of the Pinhão, Torto, Tua and Távora. Around 19,000 ha of vines, almost all on steep schist, include most of the famous Vintage Port quintas: Noval, Roêda, Bomfim, Malvedos, Cavadinha, Panascal, Cruzeiro, Seixo, La Rosa, Vale Dona Maria. Rainfall is around 650 to 700 mm, summers are hot, and the many valleys give every possible aspect, which is why blenders draw on so many quintas here. It produces the bulk of the region's top-graded A and B vineyards.","terroir":"Deep, hard schist; terraces of every era (pre-phylloxera socalcos, 1970s patamares, vinha ao alto); hot south-facing amphitheatres along the main river, cooler side-valley slopes.","producers":["Quinta do Noval","Graham's","Dow's","Warre's","Fonseca","Taylor's","Croft","Quinta do Crasto","Quinta de la Rosa","Quinta Vale Dona Maria","Wine & Soul","Quinta do Infantado","Niepoort"],"nerd_note":"Pombal's first boundary (marked out 1757 to 1761) covered roughly the Baixo Corgo and the western Cima Corgo; it crept east over the following decades, and the Douro Superior was only fully included in the early 20th century.","rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-colares":{"name":"Colares","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"1908","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Ramisco (red), Malvasia de Colares (white)","summary":"Colares is one of the strangest and most romantic wine places in Europe. On the Atlantic coast below Sintra, its DOC vines grow in deep beach sand (chão de areia) on their own roots, because phylloxera cannot survive in sand; when the louse destroyed Europe's vineyards in the late 19th century, Colares boomed. To plant, growers dig trenches several metres down through the sand until they reach clay, lay the cuttings in, and fill back. The vines sprawl along the ground, propped on small canes to keep the grapes off the hot sand, behind fences of woven cane that break the ocean wind.\n\nThe red, Ramisco, is ferociously tannic and acidic when young and needs decades; old Colares (Viúva Gomes or the Adega Regional bottlings from the 1930s to 1970s) turns into something like old Nebbiolo crossed with the sea: dried cherry, tea, mushroom, iodine. The white, Malvasia de Colares, is saline and fresh. Production has fallen from over a million litres a year around 1950 to a few tens of thousands, as holiday homes replaced vineyards, and only a handful of producers remain.","terroir":"Deep Atlantic dune sand over clay (chão de areia) for DOC wines; a few vines on the clay (chão rijo) nearby must be grafted and do not qualify for the classic style. Fog, salt wind, cool summers.","producers":["Adega Regional de Colares","Viúva Gomes","Fundação Oriente","Casal Santa Maria","Ramilo","Monte Cascas"],"nerd_note":"The cooperative, Adega Regional de Colares, held a legal monopoly on vinifying Colares wine from 1931 until the 1990s; its cask hall, fitted with tropical-wood vats of uneven sizes, still holds the region's stocks.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-cova-da-beira":{"name":"Cova da Beira","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Beira Interior","subregion":"Beira Interior","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Rufete, Tinta Roriz, Touriga Nacional, Trincadeira; Síria, Fonte Cal, Arinto","summary":"The basin of the Zêzere between the Serra da Estrela and the Serra da Gardunha, around Belmonte, Covilhã and Fundão (famous for cherries). The largest and most dynamic subregion.","terroir":"Granite and schist in a sheltered valley.","producers":["Quinta dos Termos"],"rslug":"beira-interior"},"pt-dao":{"name":"Dão","cat":"DOC","region":"Dão","created":"1908","area_ha":9827,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Alfrocheiro, Tinta Roriz, Jaen, Tinto Cão; Encruzado, Malvasia Fina, Bical, Cercial","summary":"If the Douro is Portugal's Rhône, the Dão is its Burgundy. Touriga Nacional, native here, shows a finer, more floral face than in the Douro, with violet, bergamot and red cherry, and is often blended with Alfrocheiro (bright, red-fruited), Jaen (the Mencía of Bierzo) and Tinta Roriz. The reds have fresh acidity and fine, granitic tannins, and the best age for decades: the old Garrafeiras of the 1960s from the Centro de Estudos de Nelas and cooperatives can still astonish.\n\nThe whites are the other revelation. Encruzado, almost unique to the Dão, takes barrel fermentation and lees ageing like Chardonnay, gaining a smoky, nutty, saline texture over ten years or more. Modern producers such as Álvaro Castro, Quinta dos Roques, Quinta dos Carvalhais, Casa de Mouraz, António Madeira and Textura are among Portugal's most admired.","terroir":"Decomposed granite at 400 to 700 m in a basin surrounded by mountains; warm, dry summers, cold wet winters, wide day-night swings.","producers":["Quinta dos Carvalhais (Sogrape)","Quinta dos Roques","Álvaro Castro (Quinta da Pellada, Quinta de Saes)","Casa de Santar","Quinta da Falorca","Casa de Mouraz","António Madeira","Textura (Quinta da Boavista)","Quinta do Mondego","Lusovini","Boas Quintas","Niepoort (Quinta da Lomba)"],"nerd_note":"Jaen is the same grape as Mencía in Spain's Bierzo; pilgrims on the road to Santiago are often credited with carrying it, though in which direction is still argued.","contains":["Alva","Besteiros","Castendo","Serra da Estrela","Silgueiros","Terras de Azurara","Terras de Senhorim"],"rslug":"d-o"},"pt-dotejo":{"name":"DoTejo","cat":"DOC","region":"Tejo","created":"2011 (as DoTejo; formerly Ribatejo IPR/DOC)","area_ha":12000,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","fortified"],"grapes":"Fernão Pires, Arinto, Talia; Castelão, Trincadeira, Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz, Alicante Bouschet","summary":"A big DOC along the Tagus valley. Its signature white is Fernão Pires: aromatic, citrus-and-flower scented, sometimes barrel-fermented or late-harvested. Reds range from light, fruity Castelão from the floodplain to dense Touriga and Alicante blends from the sandy charneca. Most of the region's wine is sold as IGP Tejo; the DOC is used for more traditional, regionally typical wines.","terroir":"Alluvial floodplain, sandy heath and clay-limestone hills; Mediterranean climate with Atlantic influence up the river.","producers":["Quinta da Alorna","Casal Branco","Quinta da Lagoalva","Falua","Casa Cadaval","Fiuza"],"nerd_note":"The EU register writes the name as one word, DoTejo, a contraction of 'do Tejo' (of the Tagus), so the label does not read as plain 'Tejo', which is the IGP.","rslug":"tejo"},"pt-douro":{"name":"Douro","cat":"DOC","region":"Douro Valley","created":"1756 (region); DOC for unfortified wines late 20th century","area_ha":43000,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling","fortified","sweet"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Tinto Cão, Sousão, old-vine field blends; whites from Rabigato, Viosinho, Gouveio, Códega do Larinho, Malvasia Fina, Arinto; Moscatel Galego Branco for Moscatel do Douro","summary":"The Douro DOC covers the unfortified wines of the same demarcated region as Port, and for most of its history they were an afterthought: everyday reds for the farm, or the grapes that missed out on the Port quota. The exception that proved what was possible was Barca Velha, first made in 1952 by Fernando Nicolau de Almeida at Quinta do Vale Meão for A. A. Ferreira, using ice carried up the valley to cool the fermentation. It is released only in exceptional years (around twenty times since 1952) and remains Portugal's most famous red.\n\nThe real revolution came after 1986 and especially in the 1990s, when families who had sold grapes to Port shippers began bottling dry wines under their own names. The five estates that marketed themselves together from 2003 as the Douro Boys (Quinta do Crasto, Quinta do Vale Meão, Quinta do Vallado, Niepoort and Quinta Vale Dona Maria) became the face of that change. Today more than 60 million bottles of Douro DOC wine are sold a year, using over half of the region's grapes.\n\nThe classic Douro red is dark, dense and perfumed: Touriga Nacional's violets and bergamot, Touriga Franca's rose and black fruit, the grip of schist-grown tannin, often with the herbal, resinous note of the hillside scrub (esteva, rockrose). Old-vine field blends (vinhas velhas), sometimes with 30 or more varieties interplanted, give the most complex wines. Whites from high plateau vineyards around Alijó, Murça, Favaios and the Douro Superior's upper slopes are increasingly serious: taut, saline and long-lived. The DOC also covers Moscatel do Douro, rosés and traditional-method sparkling wine.","terroir":"Same schist slopes and climate gradient as Port, but table-wine growers prize what the Port points system penalises: altitude (500 to 700 m for whites), north and east aspects, granite margins and old field-blend vineyards. The economic problem is that DOC grapes are worth far less than Port grapes, which pushes growers toward volume and has led to abandonment of steep plots.","producers":["Casa Ferreirinha (Barca Velha)","Quinta do Crasto","Quinta do Vale Meão","Niepoort","Quinta do Vallado","Quinta Vale Dona Maria","Wine & Soul","Quinta da Gaivosa (Alves de Sousa)","Quinta do Noval","Prats & Symington (Chryseia)","Quinta Nova","Real Companhia Velha","Luis Seabra","Mateus Nicolau de Almeida"],"nerd_note":"Barca Velha is named after an old boat; the first vintage was cooled with blocks of ice brought from Porto, because Vale Meão had no electricity. When a year is good but not quite great enough, Casa Ferreirinha releases it as Reserva Especial instead.","contains":["Moscatel do Douro","Baixo Corgo","Cima Corgo","Douro Superior"],"rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-douro-superior":{"name":"Douro Superior","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Douro Valley","created":"Incorporated progressively, 19th and 20th centuries","area_ha":8700,"styles":["fortified","red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz, Sousão; whites Rabigato, Códega do Larinho","summary":"East of the Cachão da Valeira gorge to the Spanish border at Barca d'Alva: the largest subregion by land but with only about 8,700 ha of vines, because it was cut off by the rapids until the 1790s and remote until the railway reached it in 1887. It is the hottest and driest part of the Douro (around 400 mm of rain near the border), with flatter land that allows mechanisation, and it is where most new planting has happened since the 1980s. Great estates include Quinta de Vargellas (Taylor's), Quinta do Vesuvio (Symington), Quinta do Vale Meão (birthplace of Barca Velha) and Quinta da Leda (Casa Ferreirinha). Wines are riper, darker and fuller, with soft, generous tannins.","terroir":"Schist and some granite; continental climate with very hot summers and cold winters; the Côa valley, with its Palaeolithic rock art, enters the Douro here.","producers":["Taylor's (Vargellas)","Quinta do Vesuvio","Quinta do Vale Meão","Casa Ferreirinha (Quinta da Leda)","Ramos Pinto (Ervamoira)","Cockburn's (Quinta dos Canais)","Dow's (Senhora da Ribeira)"],"nerd_note":"Ramos Pinto's Quinta de Ervamoira was planted in the 1970s with Portugal's first large vinha ao alto vineyard, and the discovery of rock engravings nearby in the 1990s helped stop a dam that would have flooded it.","rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-duriense":{"name":"Duriense","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Douro Valley","created":"IGP (Vinho Regional)","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Douro varieties plus international grapes","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Douro area, used for wines made outside DOC Douro rules: different varieties or blends, experimental styles, or wines a producer simply prefers not to submit to the DOC tasting panel. Some serious bottlings appear under it, but most Duriense wine is everyday drinking.","terroir":"Same area as the Douro DOC.","producers":["Casa de Mateus (Sogrape)","various Douro cooperatives"],"nerd_note":"Like the DOC, it is administered by the IVDP rather than a regional commission.","rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-encostas-d-aire":{"name":"Encostas d’Aire","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"DOP registered 1991 (former IPR)","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Baga, Castelão, Trincadeira, Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz; Arinto, Fernão Pires, Vital","summary":"A large DOC on the limestone foothills of the Serra de Aire and Serra dos Candeeiros, around Leiria, Batalha, Alcobaça, Fátima and Ourém. It has two subregions, Alcobaça and Ourém; the latter is home to Medieval de Ourém, a traditional blend of white Fernão Pires with a little red Trincadeira, fermented separately and blended, now a recognised style within the DOC. The Cistercian abbey of Alcobaça, whose monks planted vines here from the 12th century, has a wine museum.","terroir":"Limestone and clay, karst hills, Atlantic-influenced to the west and warmer inland toward the Tejo.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Ourém"],"nerd_note":"Medieval de Ourém revives a style mentioned in medieval charters: about 80% white must and 20% red, fermented apart and blended in spring.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-evora":{"name":"Évora","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Aragonez, Trincadeira, Alicante Bouschet, Touriga Nacional; Roupeiro, Arinto, Antão Vaz","summary":"Around the UNESCO-listed city of Évora, home of the Fundação Eugénio de Almeida (Cartuxa, Pêra-Manca) and of Tapada de Coelheiros (Arraiolos). Granite and schist plains.","terroir":"Granite and schist, rolling plain.","producers":["Cartuxa (Fundação Eugénio de Almeida)","Tapada de Coelheiros"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-graciosa":{"name":"Graciosa","cat":"DOC","region":"Azores","created":"1994; DOP registered 1996","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Verdelho, Arinto, Boal, Fernão Pires, Terrantez","summary":"The DOC of Graciosa, the flattest and driest of the Azores, a UNESCO biosphere reserve. Its dry whites, made mainly by the island cooperative, are fresh and saline; since 2019 the regional rules allow still, sparkling and fortified whites under all three Azores DOCs, Graciosa, Pico and Biscoitos.","terroir":"Volcanic soils on a low, windy island.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa da Graciosa"],"rslug":"azores"},"pt-granja-amareleja":{"name":"Granja-Amareleja","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Moreto, Trincadeira, Aragonez; Roupeiro, Rabo de Ovelha, Manteúdo","summary":"The hottest, driest corner of the Alentejo, on the Spanish border around Mourão and Amareleja, with poor schist soils. Old bush vines of the local red Moreto survive here.","terroir":"Thin schist, extreme heat (Amareleja often records Portugal's highest temperatures).","rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-lafoes":{"name":"Lafões","cat":"DOC","region":"Dão","created":"1991 (EU register); former IPR","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Arinto, Cercial; Amaral, Jaen, Touriga Nacional","summary":"A small, rainy hill region between the Dão and the Vinho Verde country, around São Pedro do Sul, Vouzela and Oliveira de Frades. Its wines are a transition between the two: light, high-acid whites from Arinto and Cercial and sharp reds from Amaral (Vinho Verde's Azal Tinto) and Jaen. Production is tiny and mostly local.","terroir":"Granite valleys of the Vouga, Atlantic-influenced and wet.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Lafões"],"nerd_note":"Lafões is better known in Portugal for its veal (vitela de Lafões) than its wine.","rslug":"d-o"},"pt-lagoa":{"name":"Lagoa","cat":"DOC","region":"Algarve","created":"1980s; DOP registered 1991","styles":["red","white","rose","fortified"],"grapes":"Negra Mole, Castelão, Trincadeira, Touriga Nacional, Syrah; Arinto, Síria, Malvasia Fina, Manteúdo","summary":"The Algarve's most important DOC, stretching from Lagoa and Silves east to Albufeira and Loulé. The Lagoa cooperative (founded 1945) dominated for decades; estates such as Quinta dos Vales, Morgado do Quintão (with century-old Negra Mole vines) and Adega do Cantor have raised the bar, including the region's characteristic pale, savoury Negra Mole reds and an old tradition of dry fortified wine.","terroir":"Red clay and limestone barrocal behind the coast, sheltered by the Serra de Monchique.","producers":["Adega de Lagoa","Morgado do Quintão","Quinta dos Vales","Adega do Cantor (Vida Nova)","Quinta do Francês"],"nerd_note":"Negra Mole is so pale that its reds are often mistaken for rosés; the Algarve sold them as the local 'clarete'.","rslug":"algarve"},"pt-lagos":{"name":"Lagos","cat":"DOC","region":"Algarve","created":"1980s; DOP registered 1991","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Negra Mole, Castelão, Touriga Nacional, Syrah; Arinto, Síria, Malvasia Fina","summary":"The westernmost Algarve DOC, from Lagos out to Sagres and up the Atlantic coast to Aljezur. Windswept and cooler, with a handful of small estates making fresh reds and whites.","terroir":"Sandy and limestone soils exposed to Atlantic winds.","producers":["Quinta dos Vales (nearby)","Monte da Casteleja"],"rslug":"algarve"},"pt-lima":{"name":"Lima","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Loureiro, Arinto, Trajadura; Vinhão, Borraçal","summary":"The valley of the Lima river around Ponte de Lima and Arcos de Valdevez, the homeland of Loureiro. Loureiro here is floral and citrus-bright, with laurel (louro), lime blossom and real tension. Quinta do Ameal showed in the 1990s that Lima Loureiro could age.","terroir":"Granite, an open Atlantic valley with high rainfall and mild temperatures.","producers":["Quinta do Ameal","Adega Ponte de Lima","Casa de Paço d'Anha"],"rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-lisboa":{"name":"Lisboa","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Lisboa","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Lisboa region, called Estremadura until 2008, used by much of the region's production including many of its best-known estates.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-lourinha":{"name":"Lourinhã","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Aguardente (brandy) appellation","region":"Lisboa","created":"1990s (brandy appellation)","grapes":"Alicante Branco, Boal Espinho, Malvasia Rei, Tália; Cabinda, Marufo, Tinta Miúda","summary":"Lourinhã is a demarcated region for grape brandy (aguardente vínica), one of only a handful of brandy appellations in Europe alongside Cognac, Armagnac and Jerez. Thin, very acidic base wines from the windy coast north of Lisbon are double-distilled and aged at least two years in oak; the best are aged far longer. Production is tiny, led by the local cooperative.","terroir":"Coastal clay and sandstone, cool Atlantic winds that keep base wines low in alcohol and high in acid, ideal for distillation.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa da Lourinhã"],"nerd_note":"Because it covers brandy, not wine, Lourinhã does not appear in the EU register of wine PDOs; it is protected as a spirit drink geographical indication.","rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-madeira":{"name":"Madeira","cat":"DOC","region":"Madeira","created":"Regulated from the early 20th century; DOP registered 1991; statute 2015","area_ha":400,"styles":["fortified","sweet","white","red"],"grapes":"Tinta Negra (majority); noble whites Sercial, Verdelho, Boal, Malvasia (Malvasia de São Jorge, Malvasia Cândida), Terrantez; Bastardo, Moscatel, Complexa, Listrão","summary":"Madeira is the only wine region where heat and air, the two things that ruin every other wine, are the point of the process. Grapes are pressed and fermented, then fortified with grape spirit at around 96% to between 17 and 22% alcohol: early, during fermentation, for the sweet styles (Boal, Malvasia), and later for dry Sercial and Verdelho. The wine is then either warmed in an estufa (no more than 50 °C, at least three months) or, for every fine Madeira, left in casks in hot lofts (canteiro) for years or decades. The slow cooking and oxidation turn fruit into caramel, toffee, roasted nuts, marmalade, coffee and smoke, while Madeira's ferocious acidity keeps it alive.\n\nThe labels tell you three things. The grape, if named (85% minimum), fixes the sweetness: Sercial dry, Verdelho medium dry, Boal medium sweet, Malvasia sweet, Terrantez in between. The age: 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 50 or More than 50 years for blends, or a vintage for Colheita (at least 5 years in wood) and Frasqueira (at least 20). And the house: Blandy's, Barbeito, D'Oliveiras, Henriques & Henriques, Justino's, Broadbent, Borges, Faria.\n\nGreat old Madeira is one of the longest-lived things you can drink. Wines from the 19th century, and occasionally the 18th, still appear at auction and are often in perfect condition, because there is nothing left in them to spoil. Once opened, a bottle lasts months.","terroir":"Basalt terraces from sea level to about 800 m on the south coast and lower, cooler slopes on the north; very high natural acidity everywhere. The south (Câmara de Lobos, Estreito, Campanário) is warmer and supplies Tinta Negra, Boal and Malvasia; the north (São Vicente, Seixal, Porto Moniz, Santana) gives Sercial and Verdelho their tension.","producers":["Blandy's (Madeira Wine Company)","Cossart Gordon","Barbeito","Pereira d'Oliveira (D'Oliveiras)","Henriques & Henriques","Justino's","H. M. Borges","Broadbent","J. Faria & Filhos"],"nerd_note":"Since 2015 Tinta Negra can be named on the label, and shippers now bottle 50-year-old Tinta Negra Colheitas that are as complex as noble-grape wines. The rule linking variety to sweetness can be waived for Frasqueiras with the IVBAM tasting panel's approval.","rslug":"madeira"},"pt-madeirense":{"name":"Madeirense","cat":"DOC","region":"Madeira","created":"DOP registered 2007","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Verdelho, Sercial, Arnsburger, Malvasia, Boal, Tinta Negra, Aragonez, Touriga Nacional, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Bastardo and others","summary":"The DOP for Madeira's unfortified table wines: whites from Verdelho, Sercial, Arnsburger and Malvasia, reds and rosés from Tinta Negra and mainland or international varieties. Volumes are small and mostly consumed on the island, but quality has improved fast; Atlantis rosé from the Madeira Wine Company and Verdelho-based whites from the north coast show sharp acidity and a salty edge. Sales of Madeirense DOP rose almost 24% in 2025.","terroir":"Same basalt terraces as Madeira DOP; whites favour the cool, wet north coast (São Vicente, Seixal, Porto Moniz).","producers":["Madeira Wine Company (Atlantis)","Barbeito","Quinta do Barbusano","Terras do Avô","Seiçal"],"nerd_note":"Arnsburger, a German Riesling crossing introduced in the 20th century, has become one of the island's typical table-wine whites.","rslug":"madeira"},"pt-minho":{"name":"Minho","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Vinho Verde","created":"IGP","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Vinho Verde varieties plus international grapes","summary":"The regional wine (IGP) designation covering the Vinho Verde area, used for wines outside DOC rules, such as higher-alcohol or oak-aged Alvarinhos, international varieties or experimental styles.","terroir":"As Vinho Verde.","rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-moncao-e-melgaco":{"name":"Monção e Melgaço","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Alvarinho, Trajadura; Pedral, Alvarelhão, Borraçal, Vinhão","summary":"The northernmost subregion, along the south bank of the Minho river facing Galicia's Rías Baixas, and the home of Alvarinho (Albariño across the river). Protected from Atlantic rain by the mountains to the west and north, it is warmer and drier in summer than the rest of Vinho Verde, so Alvarinho ripens to 12.5 to 13.5% while keeping its acidity. Palácio da Brejoeira made the first prestige bottling here in 1976; Soalheiro, Anselmo Mendes and the Monção cooperative made the name international. Until 2021 only wines from here could be labelled Vinho Verde Alvarinho.","terroir":"Granite with alluvial terraces near the river; a semi-continental pocket within the Atlantic north-west. Melgaço's higher, cooler vineyards (Soalheiro) give tauter wines than Monção's warmer valley floor.","producers":["Soalheiro","Anselmo Mendes","Palácio da Brejoeira","Adega de Monção","Quinta de Santiago","Quintas de Melgaço"],"nerd_note":"Wines from here may be labelled with the subregion name alone, Monção e Melgaço, without saying Alvarinho.","rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-moscatel-do-douro":{"name":"Moscatel do Douro","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Traditional designation within DOC Douro","region":"Douro Valley","subregion":"Cima Corgo","created":"Traditional; protected under DOC Douro","styles":["fortified","sweet","white"],"grapes":"Moscatel Galego Branco (Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains), at least 85%","summary":"Moscatel do Douro is the Douro's other fortified wine: Muscat grapes, fortified with grape spirit like Port, at least 85% Moscatel and 16.5% alcohol or more, then aged at least 18 months in wood. Most of it comes from the high, cooler plateau around Favaios and Alijó, at roughly 500 to 600 m, where the small-berried Moscatel Galego keeps its acidity and its orange-blossom perfume. The village cooperative, Adega Cooperativa de Favaios, made the name so well known in Portugal that many people simply order 'a Favaios'. Aged versions (10 and 20 years, occasionally single-vintage) turn amber and develop notes of orange peel, honey and walnut.","terroir":"Granite and schist plateau north of Pinhão, at the edge of the demarcated region; more Atlantic influence and cooler nights than the river valley.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Favaios","Quinta da Avessada","Poças","Quinta do Portal"],"nerd_note":"A dash of Moscatel is also used by some shippers to lift the aroma of white Port blends.","rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-moura":{"name":"Moura","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Alfrocheiro, Castelão, Moreto, Trincadeira; Antão Vaz, Fernão Pires, Rabo de Ovelha","summary":"East of the Guadiana around Moura, a town better known for olive oil; clay and limestone soils that hold water through the summer give fuller wines.","terroir":"Clay-limestone, hot and dry.","rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-obidos":{"name":"Óbidos","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"DOP registered 1991 (former IPR)","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Arinto, Fernão Pires, Vital, Seara Nova; Castelão, Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz","summary":"Around the walled medieval town of Óbidos (and its cherry liqueur, ginjinha). Cool, Atlantic-exposed hills give fresh whites and the region's best traditional-method sparkling base wines.","terroir":"Clay and limestone, strong Atlantic influence.","producers":["Companhia Agrícola do Sanguinhal"],"rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-paiva":{"name":"Paiva","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Avesso, Arinto, Loureiro; Amaral, Vinhão","summary":"South of the Douro around Castelo de Paiva and Cinfães, a smaller subregion of hillside vineyards; known for reds as well as Avesso-based whites.","terroir":"Granite and schist, south bank of the Douro.","rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-palmela":{"name":"Palmela","cat":"DOC","region":"Península de Setúbal","created":"DOP registered 1991","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling","fortified"],"grapes":"Castelão (dominant in reds), Alicante Bouschet, Touriga Nacional, Aragonez, Syrah; Fernão Pires, Arinto, Moscatel","summary":"Palmela is Castelão's home appellation, on the same ground as the Setúbal DOC but for table wines (and a fortified Moscatel too). On the sandy plain, Castelão gives soft, juicy reds with red cherry and a touch of spice; on the limestone of the Arrábida it is firmer. The castle of Palmela, a former seat of the Order of Santiago, looks over the vineyards.","terroir":"Sands of the plain and limestone of the Arrábida hills.","producers":["Casa Ermelinda Freitas","José Maria da Fonseca","Bacalhôa","Adega de Pegões"],"nerd_note":"Periquita, the José Maria da Fonseca red launched in the 1850s, was so successful that Periquita became a synonym for the Castelão grape across Portugal.","rslug":"pen-nsula-de-set-bal"},"pt-peninsula-de-setubal":{"name":"Península de Setúbal","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Península de Setúbal","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Setúbal Peninsula, used for most of its table wine.","rslug":"pen-nsula-de-set-bal"},"pt-pico":{"name":"Pico","cat":"DOC","region":"Azores","created":"1994; DOP registered 1996","styles":["fortified","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Verdelho, Arinto dos Açores, Terrantez do Pico, Boal, Sercial, Fernão Pires, Generosa, Galego Dourado, Malvasia","summary":"Pico is a single, near-perfect volcanic cone rising 2,351 m from the Atlantic, and its vineyards sit on the lava flows at its foot, below 100 m, on the west coast around Madalena, Criação Velha and Bandeiras and on the north coast at Santa Luzia. Settlers broke the black basalt into thousands of small rectangular pens (currais), each a few metres across, walled with loose stones to shelter vines from wind and salt spray; the vines root into cracks in the lava. The resulting grid, stretching for kilometres, is one of the world's great agricultural landscapes.\n\nHistorically Pico made a fortified Verdelho (licoroso), and the 2014 EU specification still defines DOC Pico around that style. Today the excitement is the dry whites, especially Arinto dos Açores and Terrantez do Pico: piercing acidity, flinty smoke, citrus and an unmistakable sea-salt finish. The Azores Wine Company, the Pico cooperative, Fita Preta and Insula are the leading producers.","terroir":"Fresh basalt lava (lajido) with almost no soil; walls of black stone that also radiate heat at night; extreme exposure to wind and salt; maritime, humid climate with high disease pressure.","producers":["Azores Wine Company","Cooperativa Vitivinícola da Ilha do Pico (Picowines)","Insula","Pico Wines","Fita Preta (Azores)","Adega A Buraca"],"nerd_note":"The currais walls, piled from stones cleared from the lava, were so extensive that, it is often said, their total length would circle the Earth twice.","rslug":"azores"},"pt-pinhel":{"name":"Pinhel","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Beira Interior","subregion":"Beira Interior","styles":["white","sparkling","red"],"grapes":"Síria, Fonte Cal, Arinto; Rufete, Marufo","summary":"Around the fortified town of Pinhel; known for whites and a long tradition of sparkling wine, with its cooperative as the main producer.","terroir":"Granite plateau at 500 to 700 m.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Pinhel"],"rslug":"beira-interior"},"pt-planalto-mirandes":{"name":"Planalto Mirandês","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Trás-os-Montes","subregion":"Trás-os-Montes","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Bastardo, Tinta Roriz, Touriga Nacional, Marufo; Síria, Malvasia Fina","summary":"The high plateau around Miranda do Douro and Mogadouro, above the Douro's international canyon, where Mirandese (Portugal's second official language) is spoken. Old field blends, often ungrafted or very old, are being rediscovered by small growers.","terroir":"Granite plateau at 600 to 800 m; extreme continental climate.","producers":["Arribas Wine Company"],"rslug":"tr-s-os-montes"},"pt-portalegre":{"name":"Portalegre","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Trincadeira, Aragonez, Grand Noir, Alicante Bouschet, old field blends; Arinto, Roupeiro, Fernão Pires","summary":"The northernmost and coolest subregion, on the granite and schist slopes of the Serra de São Mamede near the Spanish border, with vineyards up to 700 m and old field blends. It is the darling of the 'new Alentejo': fresher, lighter, more mineral wines. Symington bought Quinta da Fonte Souto here in 2017; Susana Esteban, Tapada do Chaves and Rui Reguinga are other names.","terroir":"Granite and schist, altitude 400 to 700 m, higher rainfall than the plains.","producers":["Tapada do Chaves","Quinta da Fonte Souto (Symington)","Susana Esteban","Rui Reguinga"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-portimao":{"name":"Portimão","cat":"DOC","region":"Algarve","created":"1980s; DOP registered 1991","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Negra Mole, Castelão, Trincadeira, Touriga Nacional; Arinto, Síria","summary":"The smallest Algarve DOC, around Portimão, Alvor and Mexilhoeira Grande; very little wine is bottled under the name.","terroir":"Coastal sands and clays.","rslug":"algarve"},"pt-porto":{"name":"Porto","cat":"DOC","region":"Douro Valley","created":"1756","area_ha":43000,"styles":["fortified","sweet","red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Touriga Franca, Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Tinto Cão, Tinta Amarela, Sousão and dozens more in old field blends; for white Port, Malvasia Fina, Gouveio, Viosinho, Rabigato, Códega do Larinho","summary":"Port is a red (or white) wine whose fermentation is stopped part-way by adding grape spirit. Grapes are crushed and, at the best quintas, still trodden by foot in shallow granite tanks called lagares, because Port ferments for only two or three days and colour and tannin have to be extracted fast. When about half the sugar has turned to alcohol, the wine is run off onto aguardente vínica, a neutral grape brandy of about 77% alcohol: roughly 115 litres of spirit to 435 litres of must makes a 550-litre pipa. The yeasts die, the remaining sugar stays, and the wine ends up at 19 to 22% alcohol (white Port can be as low as 16.5%).\n\nWhat happens next decides the style. Ruby Ports are aged in large vats or bottle to keep their dark fruit: basic Ruby; Reserve; Late Bottled Vintage (a single year bottled between the fourth and sixth year, a style pioneered by Taylor's with its 1965); Crusted (a bottle-aged blend of vintages); and Vintage Port, made only in the best years from the best lots, bottled unfiltered in its second or third year and built to age for decades. A shipper declares a vintage after the IVDP approves samples in the spring of the second year; famous general declarations include 1927, 1945, 1963, 1977, 1994, 2011, and the back-to-back 2016 and 2017. Single-quinta Vintage Ports (Vargellas, Malvedos, Cavadinha, Vesuvio) often come from the years in between.\n\nTawny Ports are aged in small, old casks (pipas, about 550 to 620 litres) where slow oxidation turns them amber, nutty and dried-fruited. A 10, 20, 30 or 40 Year Old Tawny is a blend whose taste matches that average age; the IVDP added 50 Years Old in 2021/22 and, by a circular of January 2025, an 80 Years Old indication plus Very Very Old (VVO) for unstated blends of 80 years or more. Colheita is a single-vintage tawny aged at least seven years in wood, labelled with harvest and bottling dates. White Port ranges from dry aperitif styles to aged whites with an indication of age, and rosé Port has been allowed since 2008.","terroir":"Only the schist core of the valley, graded A to F under the beneficio, can supply Port. The Cima Corgo, around Pinhão and its side valleys, provides most Vintage Port; the Douro Superior gives increasingly important ripe, powerful wines; the Baixo Corgo, wetter and more fertile, supplies much of the ruby and young tawny. Most Port is still aged and blended in the lodges of Vila Nova de Gaia, where the cool, humid Atlantic air slows evaporation; wines aged up the valley take on a hotter, richer 'Douro bake'.","producers":["Taylor's","Fonseca","Croft","Graham's","Dow's","Warre's","Cockburn's","Quinta do Noval","Niepoort","Ramos Pinto","Ferreira","Sandeman","Kopke","Cálem","Burmester","Quinta do Vesuvio","Quinta do Crasto","Quinta de la Rosa","Real Companhia Velha","Wine & Soul"],"nerd_note":"Vintage Port is the only great wine in the world normally released when it is two years old. A 'declaration' is a shipper's decision, not the IVDP's: the Institute only approves the samples, so in split years some houses declare and others make single-quinta wines instead.","contains":["Baixo Corgo","Cima Corgo","Douro Superior"],"rslug":"douro-valley"},"pt-redondo":{"name":"Redondo","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Aragonez, Trincadeira, Alicante Bouschet, Castelão; Roupeiro, Fernão Pires","summary":"South of Borba at the foot of the Serra d'Ossa, a subregion of warm granite and schist. Known for its cooperative and its tradition of pottery.","terroir":"Granite and schist sheltered by the Serra d'Ossa.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Redondo"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-reguengos":{"name":"Reguengos","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Aragonez, Trincadeira, Alicante Bouschet, Moreto; Roupeiro, Antão Vaz","summary":"Around Reguengos de Monsaraz, the largest subregion by production, home of Herdade do Esporão and the cooperative CARMIM. Hot, dry and schistous, it makes powerful, ripe reds. Monsaraz's hilltop village looks over the vineyards and the Alqueva lake.","terroir":"Schist and some granite, very hot summers.","producers":["Herdade do Esporão","CARMIM","José de Sousa (Reguengos)"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-serra-da-estrela":{"name":"Serra da Estrela","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Alfrocheiro, Jaen; Encruzado, Malvasia Fina","summary":"The highest Dão subregion, on the western foothills of the Serra da Estrela around Seia and Gouveia; vineyards reach about 800 m, giving the freshest, most mineral wines. Álvaro Castro's Quinta da Pellada and Quinta de Saes are here.","terroir":"Granite, high altitude, cold winters and cool nights.","producers":["Quinta da Pellada (Álvaro Castro)","Quinta de Saes"],"rslug":"d-o"},"pt-setubal":{"name":"Setúbal","cat":"DOC","region":"Península de Setúbal","created":"1907","styles":["fortified","sweet","white"],"grapes":"Moscatel de Setúbal (Muscat of Alexandria); Moscatel Roxo; Antão Vaz, Arinto, Fernão Pires, Malvasia Fina and others in minor shares","summary":"Moscatel de Setúbal is one of the world's great fortified Muscats. Muscat of Alexandria grapes are fermented briefly, fortified with grape spirit to stop the fermentation, and then left to macerate on their skins for months (often five or six), so the wine draws out apricot, orange blossom, honey and spice (at least three months, often longer). It then ages at least 18 months in wood, where it turns from gold to amber and, after decades, to a deep mahogany with notes of dried figs, coffee and burnt orange, always balanced by fresh acidity. To be labelled Moscatel de Setúbal the wine must contain at least 85% Moscatel; blends with less are simply Setúbal.\n\nMoscatel Roxo, a rare pink-skinned Muscat mutation that nearly disappeared in the 20th century, was saved largely by José Maria da Fonseca and makes a more intense, spicy wine. Most are sold at two to five years old; a small share is released at 10 or 20 years or more, and single-vintage bottlings from José Maria da Fonseca's 19th- and 20th-century stocks are among Portugal's rarest wines.","terroir":"Limestone and clay slopes of the Serra da Arrábida around Azeitão and Palmela, warm and sheltered; also sandy plains inland.","producers":["José Maria da Fonseca","Bacalhôa","Horácio Simões","Casa Ermelinda Freitas","Adega de Pegões"],"nerd_note":"The long skin contact after fortification is what sets Setúbal apart from most other fortified Muscats: the alcohol extracts aroma compounds from the skins for months.","rslug":"pen-nsula-de-set-bal"},"pt-silgueiros":{"name":"Silgueiros","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Jaen, Alfrocheiro; Encruzado","summary":"South-west of Viseu along the Dão river; warm, well-exposed slopes and some of the region's riper reds. Quinta da Falorca is the best-known estate.","terroir":"Granite slopes, slightly warmer than the plateau.","producers":["Quinta da Falorca"],"rslug":"d-o"},"pt-sousa":{"name":"Sousa","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Vinho Verde","subregion":"Vinho Verde","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Loureiro, Arinto, Trajadura, Avesso, Azal; Vinhão, Espadeiro","summary":"The Sousa valley around Penafiel, Paredes, Lousada and Felgueiras, where Aveleda, the region's biggest family firm, has its estate.","terroir":"Granite, intermediate climate.","producers":["Aveleda"],"rslug":"vinho-verde"},"pt-tavira":{"name":"Tavira","cat":"DOC","region":"Algarve","created":"1980s; DOP registered 1991","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Negra Mole, Castelão, Trincadeira; Síria, Arinto, Diagalves, Moscatel Graúdo","summary":"The eastern Algarve DOC around Tavira, Faro, Olhão and Castro Marim toward the Spanish border; a few small producers and a fading cooperative tradition.","terroir":"Limestone barrocal hills and coastal plain; the hottest, driest part of the Algarve.","rslug":"algarve"},"pt-tavora-varosa":{"name":"Távora-Varosa","cat":"DOC","region":"Távora-Varosa","created":"1989 (Varosa IPR); DOC later; EU registration 2004","styles":["sparkling","white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Malvasia Fina, Cerceal, Gouveio, Bical, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir; Touriga Nacional, Tinta Roriz","summary":"The sparkling-wine DOC of Portugal's northern Beiras, on the high plateau between the Douro and Viseu. Altitude (500 to 800 m) gives slow-ripening grapes with sharp acidity, the raw material for traditional-method Espumante aged on lees. Murganheira's cellars, cut into the granite at Ucanha, hold millions of bottles; Raposeira near Lamego is the other historic house. Still whites from Malvasia Fina and Cerceal can be very fresh.","terroir":"Granite soils, high altitude, cold winters and big diurnal swings.","producers":["Murganheira","Raposeira","Cooperativa Agrícola do Távora"],"nerd_note":"Murganheira's founder dug the cellars straight into the blue granite of the hillside, giving a constant temperature for long lees ageing without air conditioning.","rslug":"t-vora-varosa"},"pt-tejo":{"name":"Tejo","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Tejo","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Tagus valley, called Ribatejano until 2009; it covers most of the region's production.","rslug":"tejo"},"pt-terras-da-beira":{"name":"Terras da Beira","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Beira Interior","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Beira Interior area.","rslug":"beira-interior"},"pt-terras-de-azurara":{"name":"Terras de Azurara","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Jaen, Alfrocheiro; Encruzado","summary":"The Mangualde area (Azurara was the medieval name), home of Sogrape's Quinta dos Carvalhais and Quinta dos Roques.","terroir":"Granite, 400 to 550 m.","producers":["Quinta dos Carvalhais","Quinta dos Roques"],"rslug":"d-o"},"pt-terras-de-cister":{"name":"Terras de Cister","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Távora-Varosa","created":"IGP","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) name for the Távora-Varosa area, named after the Cistercian monks who planted its first vineyards.","terroir":"As Távora-Varosa.","rslug":"t-vora-varosa"},"pt-terras-de-senhorim":{"name":"Terras de Senhorim","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Dão","subregion":"Dão","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Alfrocheiro, Tinta Roriz; Encruzado","summary":"Around Nelas and Carregal do Sal, the historic core of Dão research (the Centro de Estudos Vitivinícolas at Nelas) and home of Casa de Santar.","terroir":"Granite plateau at about 400 to 500 m.","producers":["Casa de Santar"],"rslug":"d-o"},"pt-terras-do-dao":{"name":"Terras do Dão","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Dão","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for the Dão and Lafões area.","rslug":"d-o"},"pt-terras-madeirenses":{"name":"Terras Madeirenses","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Madeira","created":"IGP; statute 2017","styles":["white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Island varieties plus international grapes","summary":"The regional wine designation for Madeira and Porto Santo, for wines outside the Madeirense DOP rules.","terroir":"Madeira and Porto Santo.","rslug":"madeira"},"pt-torres-vedras":{"name":"Torres Vedras","cat":"DOC","region":"Lisboa","created":"DOP registered 1996 (former IPR)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Arinto, Fernão Pires, Vital, Seara Nova; Castelão, Tinta Roriz, Touriga Nacional","summary":"One of Portugal's largest wine-producing municipalities, long the source of light 'vinho de consumo' for Lisbon. Its DOC wines are fresh whites and light reds; the biggest producers are cooperatives and Casa Santos Lima's neighbouring estates. The town gave its name to Wellington's Lines of Torres Vedras, which stopped Masséna's invasion in 1810.","terroir":"Clay-limestone, Atlantic winds and fog.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Torres Vedras"],"rslug":"lisboa"},"pt-transmontano":{"name":"Transmontano","cat":"IGP","subcat":"Vinho Regional","region":"Trás-os-Montes","created":"IGP","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Regional and international varieties","summary":"The regional (IGP) designation for Trás-os-Montes, used for wines outside DOC rules.","terroir":"As Trás-os-Montes.","rslug":"tr-s-os-montes"},"pt-tras-os-montes":{"name":"Trás-os-Montes","cat":"DOC","region":"Trás-os-Montes","created":"2006","area_ha":4565,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling","fortified"],"grapes":"Bastardo, Marufo, Tinta Roriz, Touriga Nacional, Trincadeira; Côdega do Larinho, Fernão Pires, Gouveio, Malvasia Fina, Síria, Viosinho","summary":"A large, thinly planted DOC covering the high country north of the Douro and east of the Marão. Its best wines are field blends from old vineyards and fresh, spicy reds from Valpaços; whites from Côdega do Larinho and Síria can be bright and herbal. Production is dominated by cooperatives (Valpaços, Chaves) and a few estates such as Valle Pradinhos near Macedo de Cavaleiros, whose cool-climate reds and Riesling-inflected whites made the region's name in the 1990s.","terroir":"Granite and schist plateaus at 350 to 700 m, continental climate.","producers":["Valle Pradinhos","Adega Cooperativa de Valpaços","Casal de Valle Pradinhos","Menina d'Uva","Arribas Wine Company"],"nerd_note":"Boticas' 'vinho dos mortos' (wine of the dead) is buried in bottles under the earth, a custom said to date from villagers hiding wine from Napoleon's troops in 1809.","contains":["Chaves","Valpaços","Planalto Mirandês"],"rslug":"tr-s-os-montes"},"pt-valpacos":{"name":"Valpaços","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Trás-os-Montes","subregion":"Trás-os-Montes","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tinta Roriz, Trincadeira, Bastardo, Touriga Nacional; Síria, Gouveio","summary":"The largest and most productive subregion, around the town of Valpaços and its cooperative; fuller, warmer reds from schist and granite.","terroir":"Schist and granite at 400 to 600 m.","producers":["Adega Cooperativa de Valpaços"],"rslug":"tr-s-os-montes"},"pt-vidigueira":{"name":"Vidigueira","cat":"DOC","subcat":"Subregion","region":"Alentejo","subregion":"Alentejo","styles":["white","red","orange"],"grapes":"Antão Vaz, Roupeiro, Perrum, Manteúdo; Alicante Bouschet, Trincadeira, Tinta Grossa","summary":"Below the Vidigueira fault scarp, a 50 km escarpment that channels cooling Atlantic air over the vineyards, so this southerly subregion makes the Alentejo's best whites, especially from Antão Vaz. It is also the heartland of talha wine, around Vila de Frades and Cuba, where taverns still serve wine straight from the jar in November.","terroir":"Schist and granite at the foot of the fault scarp, with Atlantic breezes.","producers":["Herdade do Rocim","Herdade do Peso (Sogrape)","Adega Cooperativa de Vidigueira, Cuba e Alvito","Quinta do Quetzal","Honrado"],"rslug":"alentejo"},"pt-vinho-verde":{"name":"Vinho Verde","cat":"DOC","region":"Vinho Verde","created":"1908","area_ha":23000,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Alvarinho, Loureiro, Arinto (Pedernã), Trajadura, Avesso, Azal; Vinhão, Borraçal, Espadeiro, Amaral","summary":"Vinho Verde is Portugal's biggest DOC by number of growers and one of its most exported: the classic is a white of 9 to 11.5% alcohol, bone-dry to off-dry, with lime, green apple and sea-breeze freshness and sometimes a faint sparkle, originally from malolactic fermentation in bottle and now usually from a little added carbon dioxide. It is the wine Portugal drinks with grilled sardines in June.\n\nThe serious end has transformed in a generation. Varietal Alvarinho from the warm, sheltered Monção e Melgaço subregion reaches 12.5 to 13.5%, with peach, citrus and saline minerality, and some, aged on lees or in large oak, need five or ten years. Loureiro from the Lima and Cávado valleys is floral and citrus-scented; Avesso from Baião is rounder and richer; Arinto gives backbone. Reds from Vinhão, almost black and high in acid, are a regional cult. The region also makes good traditional-method sparkling wines and aguardente.","terroir":"Granite soils and Atlantic rainfall; the closer to the sea, the lighter and more acidic the wines. Inland, behind the Peneda, Gerês and Marão mountains, summers are hotter and drier.","producers":["Soalheiro","Anselmo Mendes","Quinta do Ameal","Aveleda","Palácio da Brejoeira","Quinta de Covela","Quinta da Lixa","Casa de Vilacetinho","Adega de Monção","Afros","Quinta de Soalheiro","Márcio Lopes"],"nerd_note":"The spritz in cheap Vinho Verde is now mostly injected CO2; the original prickle came from malolactic fermentation finishing in bottle, which is why old-school bottles were slightly cloudy.","contains":["Monção e Melgaço","Lima","Cávado","Ave","Basto","Sousa","Amarante","Baião","Paiva"],"rslug":"vinho-verde"}}};
